"""Rubric-aligned coursework; examples describe implemented behavior, not planned features."""
from html import escape


def diagram_notes(section, tables, table_schema):
    if section == 'er':
        relationships = []
        for t in tables:
            for c in t['columns']:
                for ref in c['references']:
                    one = (len(t['keys']) == 1 and c['primary']) or [c['name']] in t['unique']
                    relationships.append('<tr><td>'+escape(t['name']+'.'+c['name'])+'</td><td>'+escape(ref)+'</td><td>1:'+('1' if one else 'N')+'</td><td>'+('partial (nullable)' if c['nullable'] else 'total (required)')+'</td></tr>')
        return '''<h2>Chen notation and mapping (Component 3: 6 + 4 marks)</h2><p>Reference convention: Elmasri and Navathe, Fundamentals of Database Systems, ER modelling and ER-to-relational mapping. Rectangles are entity types, diamonds are relationship types, ellipses are attributes, and underlined attributes form the primary key. Cardinality labels 1/N are maximum cardinalities. A double line denotes total participation; a single line denotes partial participation. No weak entity is asserted solely because a table has a foreign key.</p><p>The overview includes every current table and every declared FK. FK role names distinguish multiple relationships to AppUser and Sample. To keep the overview readable, it shows key attributes only. The separate attribute atlas shows all non-FK descriptive attributes; this relational appendix lists every column including FKs. Repeated entity names in the atlas denote the same entity type, not new tables.</p><p>Associative entities ExperimentSample and ExperimentEquipment map experiment participation. ExperimentSample has the complete three-part key (experiment_id, sample_id, usage_role); no individual FK is unique. AncientSpecimen uses Sample.sample_id as its own PK/FK, producing a 1:1 extension with total participation on the AncientSpecimen side and partial participation on Sample. AuditLog.entity_id is polymorphic and has no declared FK; it must not acquire an invented relationship diamond.</p><h2>Relationship-to-schema traceability</h2><table><tr><th>Referencing attribute / role</th><th>Referenced key</th><th>Parent:child maximum</th><th>Child participation</th></tr>'''+''.join(relationships)+'''</table><h2>ER-to-relational rules</h2><ol><li>Strong entity: create a relation with its key and simple attributes.</li><li>1:N: put the FK on the N side; NOT NULL enforces required participation.</li><li>1:1 extension: use the parent key as child PK/FK or a unique FK.</li><li>M:N associations: use an association relation and preserve its role-specific attributes and key.</li></ol><h2>Complete relational schemas</h2>'''+''.join(table_schema(t) for t in tables)
    return '''<h2>DFD notation and system boundary (Component 5: 6 + 4 marks)</h2><p>Squares represent external entities, circles represent numbered processes, parallel lines represent data stores and labelled arrows represent data flows. Context level 0 contains one process, 0 SampleMind, and no internal stores. Level 1 decomposes the same boundary into six processes. These are data flows, not screen navigation, control flow, table relationships or a transaction sequence.</p><h2>Balanced external flows</h2><table><tr><th>External party</th><th>Input to SampleMind</th><th>Output from SampleMind</th><th>Level 1 owner</th></tr><tr><td>Lab user (all roles)</td><td>Credentials</td><td>Session / access result</td><td>1.0 Access</td></tr><tr><td>Lab user</td><td>Lab records / assignments / messages</td><td>Saved records / incident updates</td><td>2.0 Operations</td></tr><tr><td>Lab user</td><td>Query / cohort criteria</td><td>Rows / coverage explanations</td><td>3.0 Query</td></tr><tr><td>Lab user</td><td>Report / chat request</td><td>Report / receipt / AI text</td><td>4.0 Reports and chat</td></tr><tr><td>Lab user</td><td>Material upload / library request</td><td>Preview / original download</td><td>5.0 Coursework</td></tr><tr><td>Lab user</td><td>News filters</td><td>Article / publisher-country results</td><td>6.0 News</td></tr><tr><td>AI provider</td><td>Assistant response</td><td>Explicit chat / report context</td><td>4.0</td></tr><tr><td>News provider</td><td>Article metadata</td><td>News query</td><td>6.0</td></tr></table><h2>Data dictionary</h2><p>D1 Accounts = AppUser identity, role and active state. D2 Lab SQL = specimen, lineage, storage/history, quantity, assay, archaeological, experiment/equipment/result, restriction, incident, analysis/model and audit relations. D3 Evidence = TinyDB custody events, readings and report receipts. D4 Materials = TinyDB coursework files and generated-material visibility metadata.</p><p>1.0 reads account records and supplies the validated identity used by protected routes. 2.0 reads/writes operational records and produces event projections. 3.0 reads SQL and evidence without changing inventory. 4.0 reads records/evidence and writes report receipts. 5.0 manages library documents and previews. 6.0 obtains optional live article metadata. External providers do not directly read a data store.</p><h2>Scope and verification</h2><p>Context arrows are decomposed without adding or dropping an external input/output. Each level 1 process has input and output; stores connect only to processes. Level 2 is optional in the supplied rubric and is intentionally omitted. Authentication is a cross-cutting precondition; the DFD does not imply that each endpoint repeats a separate network login.</p>'''


def section_body(section, tables, table_schema):
    if section == 'requirements':
        functional = [
            ('FR01', 'Authentication', 'Username/password sign-in; inactive accounts cannot authenticate. Protected routes check the current SQL account and role.', 'Missing/invalid token returns 401; forbidden role returns 403.'),
            ('FR02', 'Specimen inventory', 'Register, inspect and edit samples with type, status, quantity and optional storage references.', 'Unique codes and foreign keys remain valid after a successful write.'),
            ('FR03', 'Derivation and quantity', 'Create parent-child lineage and quantity transactions; reject cycles and stock overdraw.', 'A failed derivation leaves balances and lineage unchanged.'),
            ('FR04', 'Storage and assays', 'Record storage history and specimen tests separately from current specimen details.', 'History remains queryable after a move; multiple assays can belong to one sample.'),
            ('FR05', 'Experiments', 'Associate specimens and equipment with experiments and record results.', 'Referenced entities must exist; result codes are unique.'),
            ('FR06', 'Incidents', 'Report incidents; assign staff, accept assignments and exchange incident messages through authorized routes.', 'Only permitted actors can change an incident or access its discussion.'),
            ('FR07', 'Database demonstrations', 'Provide parameterized SQL examples, administrator SQL console, and TinyDB evidence queries.', 'Returned rows agree with persisted records; failed SQL batches roll back.'),
            ('FR08', 'Cohort evidence', 'Evaluate assay coverage and explain missing or failing evidence from stored records.', 'Every included specimen satisfies the configured requirement rule.'),
            ('FR09', 'Reports and analysis', 'Download summaries and integrity receipts; show model provenance and synthetic holdout results.', 'No real-world model accuracy claim is inferred from synthetic evaluation.'),
            ('FR10', 'Coursework library', 'Seven DBMS subtabs; preview/download materials, upload originals, soft-remove and restore.', 'Synopsis stays student-supplied; original downloads preserve uploaded bytes.'),
        ]
        rows = ''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+escape(cell)+'</td>' for cell in row)+'</tr>' for row in functional)
        return '''<h2>1. Purpose, users and boundary</h2><p>SampleMind is a classroom laboratory information system for biological specimens, storage, lineage, assays, experiments and incident handling. Its database demonstrations connect operational records to relational and document-database concepts. It is not a clinically validated decision system.</p>
<p>Actors: administrator, researcher, technician, reviewer and viewer. SQL operational records are authoritative. TinyDB contains flexible evidence documents and coursework attachments; it is an embedded document database, not a distributed cluster.</p>
<h2>2. Software and hardware requirements (rubric: 2 marks)</h2><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td>Runtime</td><td>Python 3.11+; Node.js 20+ for building the React/TypeScript frontend; FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, SQLite and TinyDB. Exact package ranges are in requirements.txt and frontend/package.json.</td></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>Current desktop browser with JavaScript, SVG and file-download support.</td></tr><tr><td>Classroom baseline</td><td>Dual-core CPU, 8 GB RAM and about 2 GB free space for dependencies and demonstration data. This is a practical recommendation, not a measured minimum.</td></tr><tr><td>Network</td><td>Core inventory and coursework run locally. Optional AI/news require internet and provider configuration.</td></tr><tr><td>Persistence</td><td>SQLite database and configured TinyDB JSON file remain inside the project. Back up both together while the service is stopped.</td></tr></table>
<h2>3. Functional requirements (rubric: 2 marks)</h2><table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Area</th><th>Required behavior</th><th>Acceptance criterion</th></tr>'''+rows+'''</table>
<h2>4. Nonfunctional requirements</h2><table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement and verification</th></tr><tr><td>NFR01 Integrity</td><td>PK, FK, unique and check constraints; transactional inventory changes. Verify rejection and rollback cases, not only successful inserts.</td></tr><tr><td>NFR02 Security</td><td>JWT authentication, server role checks, password hashes, bound parameters in fixed queries, sanitized and size-limited uploads. Verify 401/403 cases.</td></tr><tr><td>NFR03 Usability</td><td>Visible named subtabs, loading/error/empty states, keyboard-focusable actions and readable zoomable diagrams. Verify all seven sections without depending on a successful catalog fetch.</td></tr><tr><td>NFR04 Reliability</td><td>Recoverable material removal; atomic TinyDB snapshot replacement under a file lock. SQL and TinyDB do not form one distributed ACID transaction; projections may need replay.</td></tr><tr><td>NFR05 Maintainability</td><td>Schema tables and FK mappings are derived from SQLAlchemy metadata. Run coursework tests after schema changes. SQLite is the currently tested engine; a future MySQL migration requires separate validation.</td></tr><tr><td>NFR06 Performance</td><td>Bounded query responses and indexes where defined; inspect query plans before claiming an optimization. No unsupported response-time guarantee.</td></tr></table>
<h2>5. Data requirements and assumptions</h2><p>SampleType and StorageLocation own their descriptions; samples reference them. Events retain their historical values. Assay labels can recur with different results. JSON fields are intentional structured payloads, not replacements for core relational keys. Demonstration data and synthetic ML labels are not patient data.</p>
<h2>6. Syllabus traceability</h2><p>ER model and mapping: ER subtab. Keys, constraints, joins, selection, grouping and division: Tests. FDs, decomposition and normal forms: Normalized tables. Transactions and concurrency boundaries: Security and Tests. Document aggregates and nested queries: NoSQL test options. Hadoop, sharding and replication are syllabus background and are not claimed as implemented.</p>
<h2>7. File maintenance (rubric: 1 mark)</h2><p>This generated document is the default library material. Replacement documents can be uploaded, previewed, downloaded, removed and restored. Keep your approved synopsis in its separate upload-only subtab.</p>'''
    if section == 'tests':
        from .queries import QUERIES
        sql = ''
        for i, (key, (title, concepts, _, parameter, statement)) in enumerate(QUERIES.items(), 1):
            expected = {'inventory': 'One row per specimen type, including types with zero specimens.', 'storage': 'One row per active location; disposed, missing and depleted samples do not occupy slots.', 'assays': 'Only assays belonging to the chosen sample, newest requests first.', 'ancestry': 'Unique ancestors reached through parent-child edges; the starting sample is not added unless invalid cyclic data exists.', 'division': 'Samples with at least one completed passing historical assay for every required type. This query is not the latest-evidence cohort rule.', 'exceptions': 'Open/investigating incidents with a storage reference, newest first.'}[key]
            sql += f'<h3>SQL {i}. {escape(title)}</h3><p>{escape(concepts)}. Expected: {expected}</p>'
            if parameter:
                sql += '<p>In the parameterized Database Lab supply a real sample code. In the raw SQL console replace :sample_code with a quoted code such as \'DEMO-001\'; that code is illustrative, not guaranteed to exist.</p>'
            sql += '<pre>'+escape(statement)+';</pre>'
        return '''<h2>1. Test procedure and marking</h2><p>Component 4 allocates 5 marks for correct query write-up, 10 for SQL execution and 5 for NoSQL execution. The six SQL examples and six TinyDB operations below supply executable statements, concepts and expected behavior. Execute against your own demonstration database and retain actual results; this document does not invent execution screenshots or marks.</p><p>Use a backed-up demonstration database for write tests. Fixed SQL examples use bound parameters. The raw SQL console is administrator-only and can change the live application database.</p>'''+sql+'''
<h3>SQL 7. Transaction rollback without permanent modification</h3><pre>BEGIN;
UPDATE "Sample" SET notes = 'rollback demonstration'
WHERE sample_id = (SELECT MIN(sample_id) FROM "Sample");
SELECT sample_id, notes FROM "Sample" ORDER BY sample_id LIMIT 1;
ROLLBACK;
SELECT sample_id, notes FROM "Sample" ORDER BY sample_id LIMIT 1;</pre><p>Run in a SQLite command-line session on a backup. Expected: the temporary note is visible inside the transaction; the original value returns after rollback. The web console wraps its own batch transaction; do not nest this BEGIN/ROLLBACK script there.</p>
<h2>2. NoSQL execution: isolated TinyDB fixture</h2><p>Run this complete Python script from the project environment. TinyDB MemoryStorage avoids changing operational evidence. The nested subject and sample_ids demonstrate document aggregates, nested-path selection and array membership. Six assertions provide exact expected results.</p><pre>from tinydb import TinyDB, Query
from tinydb.storages import MemoryStorage

db = TinyDB(storage=MemoryStorage)
events = db.table('custody_events')
events.insert_multiple([
    {'event_id': 'E1', 'action': 'REGISTER',
     'subject': {'type': 'Sample', 'code': 'DEMO-001'},
     'sample_ids': [1], 'details': {'quantity': 5}},
    {'event_id': 'E2', 'action': 'DERIVE',
     'subject': {'type': 'Sample', 'code': 'DEMO-002'},
     'sample_ids': [1, 2], 'details': {'quantity': 1}}
])
Q = Query()
# N1: collection retrieval
assert len(events.all()) == 2
# N2: nested attribute predicate
assert len(events.search(Q.subject.type == 'Sample')) == 2
# N3: array membership
assert len(events.search(Q.sample_ids.any([2]))) == 1
# N4: selection and projection
assert [x['event_id'] for x in events.search(Q.action == 'DERIVE')] == ['E2']
# N5: update a nested payload, then read it
events.update({'details': {'quantity': 2}}, Q.event_id == 'E2')
assert events.get(Q.event_id == 'E2')['details']['quantity'] == 2
# N6: deletion with a predicate
events.remove(Q.event_id == 'E2')
assert events.count(Q.subject.code == 'DEMO-002') == 0
db.close()
print('6 NoSQL checks passed')</pre>
<h2>3. Integrity, security and library test matrix</h2><table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Action</th><th>Expected result</th></tr><tr><td>I1</td><td>Insert duplicate specimen code / unknown FK</td><td>Rejected; no invalid row persists.</td></tr><tr><td>I2</td><td>Derive more than available stock / create lineage cycle</td><td>Rejected; parent balance and graph remain unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>I3</td><td>Fail the second statement of an administrator SQL batch</td><td>Whole batch rolls back.</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>Read coursework without token; remove as viewer</td><td>401 then 403.</td></tr><tr><td>L1</td><td>Upload original synopsis, preview and download</td><td>Safe preview; download equals original bytes.</td></tr><tr><td>L2</td><td>Remove then restore generated/uploaded material</td><td>Hidden while removed; restored content is accessible.</td></tr><tr><td>L3</td><td>Upload script-bearing SVG or oversize file</td><td>400 validation response, no active content stored.</td></tr></table><h2>4. Reproducible automated checks</h2><pre>.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest backend/tests/test_coursework.py -q</pre><p>Record environment, date, test input, actual result and pass/fail when presenting. Automated API tests do not substitute for inspecting the browser or testing a configured external provider.</p>'''
    if section == 'normalization':
        schemas = ''.join(table_schema(t) for t in tables)
        return '''<h2>1. Dependencies before decomposition</h2><p>Worked teaching example: a joined export R(sample_id, test_sequence, sample_code, sample_name, sample_type_id, type_code, type_name, test_type, test_status). test_sequence is a local sequence within each sample, introduced only for this example; it is not a column in the implemented SampleTest table.</p><p>Assume candidate key (sample_id, test_sequence). Functional dependencies: sample_id determines sample_code, sample_name and sample_type_id; sample_code determines sample_id; sample_type_id determines type_code and type_name; type_code determines sample_type_id; (sample_id, test_sequence) determines test_type and test_status. Each RHS can be split into singleton dependencies for a minimal cover. No test_type-to-result dependency is assumed.</p>
<h2>2. UNF to 1NF to 2NF to 3NF</h2><div class="flow">UNF: one sample with a repeating list of tests<br>↓ flatten the repeating list; one scalar test occurrence per row<br>1NF: R with key (sample_id, test_sequence)<br>↓ remove sample_id → specimen descriptors (partial dependency)<br>2NF: SampleExport(sample_id, sample_code, sample_name, sample_type_id, type_code, type_name)<br>and TestExport(sample_id, test_sequence, test_type, test_status)<br>↓ remove sample_type_id → type_code, type_name (transitive dependency)<br>3NF: SampleExport(sample_id, sample_code, sample_name, sample_type_id)<br>+ SampleType(sample_type_id, type_code, type_name)<br>+ TestExport(sample_id, test_sequence, test_type, test_status)</div>
<p>Each split is lossless: the intersection is sample_id in the first split, then sample_type_id in the second, and each intersection determines one decomposed relation. The listed FDs can be checked in individual output relations, so this example is dependency-preserving. Alternate keys sample_code and type_code remain unique. The implementation uses globally unique sample_test_id in place of the illustrative local test_sequence.</p>
<h2>3. Normal forms of the implemented relations</h2><p>1NF: each SQL cell has one value in its declared domain. JSON fields are treated as single structured payloads; internal array members are not relational lookup attributes. 2NF: all non-key attributes depend on the entire candidate key. 3NF: no assumed non-key determinant transitively determines another non-key attribute. BCNF: every nontrivial FD determinant must be a superkey. Primary keys alone do not prove this last statement.</p>
<p>Domain assumptions: names, statuses, manufacturers, assay labels and timestamps do not uniquely determine other attributes. Event balances, units and snapshots are facts at their recorded time, not copies of current lookup descriptions. model_version uniquely identifies a registry row; AIAnalysis stores a registry FK rather than repeating model metadata. AuditLog.entity_id is a polymorphic identifier and is not a declared FK.</p>
<p>ExperimentSample has composite key (experiment_id, sample_id, usage_role). Quantity, unit, timestamp and notes describe that specific usage-role association. Neither pair nor an individual FK is assumed to determine these values. All other current relations use a single-column PK; declared non-null unique keys are alternate determinants. Under these explicitly stated dependencies, the tables satisfy 3NF/BCNF. Additional real-world FDs would require reassessment, not an automatic BCNF claim.</p>
<h2>4. Live relation-by-relation evidence</h2>'''+''.join('<h3>'+escape(t['name'])+'</h3><p>Primary determinant: '+escape(', '.join(t['keys']))+' → all remaining row attributes. Alternate declared unique sets: '+escape('; '.join(', '.join(u) for u in t['unique']) or 'none')+'. Foreign keys are inclusion constraints, not extra within-row FDs.</p>' for t in tables)+'''<h2>5. Actual relational schema</h2><p>PK, nullable/required, data types and referenced keys below come directly from the application metadata. The interactive explorer above the document follows the same schema. Review the assumptions above alongside these declarations.</p>'''+schemas
    if section == 'security':
        return '''<h2>1. Forms and modern tools (rubric: 2 marks)</h2><p>React/TypeScript implements the interface; FastAPI validates requests; SQLAlchemy applies relational operations; SQLite enforces database constraints; TinyDB stores variable document evidence. Client validation improves feedback; server validation remains authoritative.</p><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Inputs</th><th>Validation and expected behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Login</td><td>Username, password</td><td>Required credentials; hashed password comparison and active account check. Invalid login is rejected; password is never echoed.</td></tr><tr><td>Specimen</td><td>Code, name, type, quantity, unit, status, optional storage</td><td>Unique code; valid FK references and allowed values; quantity constraints. Errors must leave no partial inventory event.</td></tr><tr><td>Derivation</td><td>Parent, child data, transferred quantity</td><td>Existing parent, no cycle or insufficient stock. Child, relationship and debit commit atomically.</td></tr><tr><td>Storage move</td><td>Sample, destination, reason</td><td>Active destination; current location update and history entry stay consistent.</td></tr><tr><td>Assay / experiment result</td><td>Referenced sample/experiment, status, values, units</td><td>Existing reference; typed values; separate records support repeated measurements.</td></tr><tr><td>Incident</td><td>Type, severity, description, affected records, assignment</td><td>Valid references and authorized actor; discussion access follows incident permissions.</td></tr><tr><td>Coursework upload</td><td>Section and original file</td><td>Maximum 5 MB; supported extension and content checks; HTML/SVG sanitized; DOCX safe text preview; recoverable removal.</td></tr></table>
<h2>2. GUI and validation (rubric: 2 marks)</h2><p>The DBMS page has seven persistent named subtabs, a generated-material selector, an original-upload library, explicit active/removed views, download actions and diagram zoom. Loading, errors and empty synopsis states are distinct. Admin/researcher material management is enforced on the server; hiding a button alone is not authorization.</p>
<h2>3. Security boundaries</h2><ul><li>Authentication: bearer JWT is verified and the current active user is loaded from SQL.</li><li>Authorization: protected write routes use server-side role dependencies. Coursework uploads and generated-material removal/restore require ADMIN or RESEARCHER; uploaded files are managed by owner or administrator.</li><li>SQL injection: fixed queries bind parameter values. The administrator SQL console intentionally executes SQL; it is a privileged operation, not an untrusted public search endpoint.</li><li>Uploads: original bytes are stored in TinyDB. Safe preview and original download are distinct; active SVG content and invalid file signatures are rejected. A restrictive content policy isolates previews.</li><li>Secrets: do not place provider API keys in public materials or reports. Local classroom HTTP is not a production HTTPS deployment.</li><li>Integrity: SQL constraints and transactions protect operational rows. TinyDB uses a file lock and atomic snapshot replacement. There is no distributed transaction across the two stores.</li><li>Report receipts: local hash chaining can detect changes relative to a trusted receipt. It is not a public blockchain or independent notarization; an actor who controls both report and receipt can replace both.</li></ul>
<h2>4. Transaction and concurrency explanation</h2><div class="flow">Request validation → role check → begin SQL unit of work → validate current records / stock → apply related changes → commit<br>Any error → rollback → unchanged committed SQL state</div><p>Atomicity: related SQL writes succeed together. Consistency: constraints and domain checks preserve invariants. Isolation: SQLite serializes writers; concurrent updates require conflict handling. Durability: committed data persists in the database file. Do not describe SQLite as an implemented two-phase-locking or distributed replication demonstration.</p>
<h2>5. Validation checklist and evidence</h2><p>Test invalid credentials, absent token, viewer writes, duplicate codes, nonexistent references, negative/overdrawn quantities, lineage cycles, invalid attachments, original download equality and remove/restore. The Tests subtab contains reproducible checks. Record actual observed output rather than pre-filling a pass result.</p>
<h2>6. File maintenance and viva (rubric: 2 + 4 marks)</h2><p>Maintain this document with the actual implementation. Be able to explain why authorization is server-side, why a rollback protects stock, why a foreign key differs from a functional dependency, and why SQL/TinyDB integration is not a distributed ACID transaction. No clinical validation or production security certification is claimed.</p>'''
    raise ValueError(section)
